import { course } from './build.js';

const HOSA=['HOSA Human Growth & Development: 2026–27 blueprint','https://hosa.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/HGD-26-27.pdf'];
const CDC=['CDC: Healthy habits and child development','https://www.cdc.gov/child-development/about/index.html'];
const MILE=['CDC: Developmental milestones and screening distinction','https://www.cdc.gov/act-early/milestones/index.html'];
const PSY=['OpenStax Psychology: Lifespan development','https://openstax.org/books/psychology/pages/9-introduction'];
const PRE=['NICHD: Prenatal care','https://www.nichd.nih.gov/health/topics/pregnancy/conditioninfo/prenatal-care'];
const TEEN=['NIMH: The teen brain','https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/the-teen-brain-7-things-to-know'];
const AGE=['National Institute on Aging: Cognitive health and older adults','https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/brain-health/cognitive-health-and-older-adults'];
const GRIEF=['National Cancer Institute: Grief, bereavement, and coping with loss','https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/advanced-cancer/caregivers/planning/bereavement-pdq'];
const q=(prompt,correct,a,b,c,why)=>[prompt,[correct,a,b,c],why];
const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const sort=(title,buckets,items)=>({t:'w',kind:'sort',title,buckets,items});
const match=(title,pairs)=>({t:'w',kind:'match',title,pairs});
const lessons=[];

lessons.push({
title:'Development in context: families, culture and the lifespan',
hook:'Two children of the same age enter a clinic. One answers questions quickly in English; the other answers richly in a home language but becomes quiet when a stranger speaks English. A useful developmental assessment begins by understanding the setting, not by ranking the children.',
objectives:['Distinguish growth, maturation and development across interacting domains.','Explain how family relationships and cultural contexts influence development.','Use an ecological perspective without treating group averages as individual destinies.','Separate observation, interpretation and further assessment.'],
sections:[
['Growth is one part of development',[
'**Growth** describes measurable physical change, such as increasing height or changes in body composition. **Development** includes changes in capacities, relationships, thinking and adaptation. **Maturation** refers to biologically organized changes that unfold with time, but it does not mean experience is irrelevant. Learning to walk depends on developing strength and nervous-system coordination as well as opportunities for safe movement. Vocabulary expands through biological readiness and interactions with people who communicate. These processes overlap; a useful explanation identifies their connections rather than assigning every behavior exclusively to nature or nurture.',
'Four domains organize observation: physical or biophysical, cognitive, social and emotional. A child who reaches for a caregiver shows movement, recognition, attachment-related behavior and emotion at the same time. An adult learning a new job uses attention, existing knowledge, social feedback and physical stamina. Development therefore continues throughout life. Some capacities improve while others become more effortful, and people can use new strategies to accomplish familiar goals. A change in performance can reflect the environment rather than a change in underlying capacity: unfamiliar language, pain, poor lighting or an inaccessible task can make someone appear less capable than they are.',
{t:'w',kind:'lifespan',title:'Explore development across the lifespan'},
'Read the timeline as an illustration of broad patterns. Age categories organize a textbook; they do not establish a deadline for achievement or permission to dismiss a concern. Compare a person with their own history, the demands of the task and appropriate assessment information.'] ],
['Family relationships as developmental environments',[
'A family can provide emotional security, practical care, language, expectations and opportunities to practice skills. Families may include biological relatives, adoptive parents, foster caregivers, stepparents, chosen family and multigenerational households. Household structure alone does not establish the quality of care. Ask who provides daily support, who makes decisions and which relationships the person trusts. A grandparent who attends every appointment may hold important knowledge even when another adult has legal decision-making authority. Respectful practice distinguishes support roles from consent authority rather than assuming they are identical.',
'Predictable, responsive interactions help a child learn what to expect from others. A caregiver can acknowledge frustration, maintain a safety limit and offer a manageable choice. This supports regulation without requiring the child to suppress every feeling. Family interactions are also reciprocal: an infant with difficult sleep may affect caregiver exhaustion, which can affect responsiveness. Assessing only the infant or only the caregiver misses this cycle. Practical support, respite and accessible services may improve the relationship more effectively than blame. Protective relationships matter during adulthood as well, particularly when someone experiences illness, migration, caregiving demands or bereavement.',
'In a case analysis, identify both a challenge and an existing strength. A household facing food insecurity may also have strong communication and a reliable neighbor. Strengths do not cancel unmet needs, and unmet needs do not erase strengths. A plan should build on what works while addressing the concrete obstacle.'] ],
['Culture, language and belonging',[
'Culture includes shared meanings, practices, values and ways of relating, but individuals participate in multiple communities and interpret traditions differently. Expectations about independence, eye contact, emotional expression and responsibility may vary within and between families. A student who avoids direct eye contact may be showing respect, discomfort or distraction; the behavior alone does not reveal the explanation. Cultural humility means asking rather than presuming, reflecting on professional assumptions and remaining willing to revise an interpretation. It does not require accepting an unsafe practice without discussion.',
'Language access is essential to a fair assessment. Everyday conversational ability in a second language is not the same as understanding technical health vocabulary. Use qualified language support when needed, direct communication to the person and confirm understanding in a respectful way. Avoid treating bilingual experience as evidence of a disorder. A meaningful developmental concern requires consideration of communication across the languages and settings the person actually uses. The opening case therefore calls for information about the child’s home communication and an appropriate assessment context before drawing conclusions about cognition.',
'Belonging also affects participation. A classroom that represents diverse families can make it easier for children to discuss their experiences. An older adult whose identity is respected may feel safer sharing concerns. Discrimination can create stress and restrict opportunities, so an apparent individual difficulty may partly reflect a social barrier. The response should address the barrier rather than labeling the person as deficient.'] ],
['Ecological reasoning and a fair observation',[
'An ecological perspective connects the person with nearby relationships and broader institutions. The immediate setting includes home, school, work and peers. Connections between settings matter: a teacher and caregiver who share strategies may provide consistent support. A caregiver’s unpredictable work schedule can affect a child even though the child never enters that workplace. Public expectations, policies and historical events also shape opportunities. The same behavior may take on different significance after a move, a family illness or a major disruption in schooling.',
'Suppose a child stops joining group reading after moving schools. Record the observable behavior first: the child sits away from the circle and does not volunteer. Then consider competing explanations, including unfamiliar language, hearing difficulty, anxiety, exclusion or a mismatch in reading difficulty. Gather information across settings and ask the child in an accessible way. A single observation cannot distinguish these causes. Developmental reasoning becomes stronger when it explains what additional evidence would change the interpretation, rather than presenting an unsupported label as a conclusion.',
sort('Separate observation from interpretation',['Observation','Interpretation needing evidence'],[['The child used three-word phrases during play.',0],['The child is lazy because they were quiet.',1],['The caregiver reports a recent school change.',0],['One missed answer proves low intelligence.',1],['The child pointed to request a toy.',0],['The family does not value education.',1]]),
'The HOSA blueprint gives government, cultural and family influences substantial weight. Study these influences as mechanisms: identify what changes, how that change affects a developmental opportunity and what evidence supports the connection. This is more useful than memorizing a list of family types or cultural stereotypes.'] ]],
takeaways:['Development is multidimensional and continues across life.','Family structure does not determine relationship quality.','A fair interpretation considers language, context, strengths and alternative explanations.'],
terms:[['Maturation','Biologically organized change interacting with experience.'],['Ecological perspective','Understanding development within connected relationships, institutions and historical contexts.']],
mcqs:[q('Which example most clearly represents growth?','An increase in measured height','Learning a negotiation strategy','Developing trust in a caregiver','Understanding another viewpoint','Growth is measurable physical change; the other examples describe additional developmental domains.'),q('A child speaks little during an unfamiliar-language interview. What is the strongest next step?','Gather information about communication in familiar languages and settings','Diagnose a language disorder immediately','Ignore all caregiver observations','Compare only with fluent classmates','Context and language access are necessary before interpreting performance.'),q('Which statement best represents lifespan development?','Gains, losses and adaptation can occur throughout life','Development ends after puberty','Every ability improves continuously','All adults change at the same pace','Development is lifelong and multidirectional.'),q('Which observation is least inferential?','The child left the group after the instructions were spoken','The child dislikes learning','The family causes the problem','The child is deliberately disrespectful','It reports behavior without assigning an unverified cause.'),q('Which family factor is most useful to assess?','The availability and quality of reliable support','Whether every caregiver is biologically related','Whether the household matches one preferred structure','Whether the family never disagrees','Relationships and resources matter more than a stereotyped household form.'),q('A parent’s changing work shifts disrupt childcare. Which explanation is ecological?','Workplace conditions influence the child indirectly through family routines','Only the child’s genes can explain the change','A workplace cannot influence a child who never visits it','The child must be choosing instability','Developmental contexts can have indirect effects.'),q('Which response demonstrates cultural humility?','Ask what a practice means to this family before interpreting it','Assume all members of a cultural group agree','Treat every difference as a deficit','Avoid discussing any safety concern','Humility requires curiosity and revision of assumptions while retaining safety responsibilities.'),q('Why consider strengths alongside barriers?','Existing supports can help make an intervention feasible','Strengths prove that services are unnecessary','Barriers are always imagined','A strength cancels every risk','A balanced assessment can address needs while building on effective supports.')],
frq:{prompt:'A bilingual child becomes quiet after changing schools. The caregiver works changing shifts, but a grandparent provides consistent support. Explain three plausible influences and outline a fair assessment approach.',rubric:[['Distinguish observation from inference',['observation','assume','evidence']],['Connect family and language context',['language','caregiver','grandparent']],['Gather information across settings',['home','school','assessment']]],model:'Quiet behavior is an observation, not proof of a cognitive or language disorder. The child may be adapting to a new language environment, unfamiliar peers or disrupted routines. The grandparent is an existing source of continuity. I would ask about communication at home and school, provide qualified language support, gather the child’s perspective and consider hearing or other concerns when indicated. This uses several settings and avoids attributing the behavior to family structure or culture alone.'},sources:[HOSA,CDC,PSY]
});

lessons.push({
title:'Government, resources and opportunities to develop',
hook:'A family misses a developmental evaluation because the bus route ends before the clinic closes. Calling them unmotivated does not solve the scheduling problem. Developmental support requires understanding how resources, institutions and policy shape everyday choices.',
objectives:['Explain pathways linking social conditions to developmental opportunities.','Distinguish universal, targeted and individualized supports.','Recognize access barriers without inferring motivation.','Evaluate a support plan using meaningful outcomes.'],
sections:[
['From social conditions to daily experience',[
'Social determinants of health are conditions in which people live, learn, work and age. For development, the important question is how these conditions change a person’s daily experiences. Stable housing can support regular sleep and continuity in school. Reliable transport can make prenatal visits or rehabilitation possible. Safe play spaces create opportunities for movement and social interaction. Food access influences energy and the ability to participate. These are pathways, not guarantees: people facing adversity can thrive, and people with abundant resources can still experience developmental difficulties.',
'A useful causal chain separates an upstream condition from an immediate mechanism and an outcome. For example, an unstable work schedule may reduce predictable childcare; inconsistent arrangements may disrupt routines; the child may arrive tired and have difficulty sustaining attention. The observation does not prove the whole chain. Ask which links are supported and what competing causes, such as illness or an unsuitable classroom task, remain possible. This prevents a broad term such as poverty from becoming a substitute for actual analysis.',
'Risk factors increase the likelihood of an outcome; they do not diagnose a person. Protective factors can reduce exposure, improve coping or increase access to help. The same resource may serve several functions: a trusted community center can provide meals, social connection and referrals. A developmental plan should identify which function is needed and whether the person can actually use the resource.'] ],
['What public systems can change',[
'Government influences development through education, public health, environmental protections, income support, disability services and the organization of health care. These systems operate at different levels and may have different eligibility rules. For a HOSA question, understand the purpose of a service before memorizing an administrative detail. A school support addresses educational participation; a nutrition program addresses food-related needs; a public-health program may reduce exposure across an entire population. These services can complement one another without being interchangeable.',
'Universal supports are offered broadly, such as accessible public education or community information. Targeted supports focus on a group with a particular need. Individualized supports respond to the goals and barriers of one person. Universal design can reduce the need for special arrangements by making environments accessible from the start. Yet equal availability does not ensure equal access. A website may be public but unusable without internet access or readable language. An appointment may be technically available but impossible for someone who cannot leave work at that time.',
'In the United States, early intervention and school services have formal pathways for evaluation and support. Eligibility, procedures and local implementation should be checked through current official sources rather than assumed from a general lesson. A referral is an invitation to assessment, not a diagnosis. Families should understand what the evaluation is for, what information is requested and how they can participate. A professional should not promise a specific benefit before confirming the program’s requirements.'] ],
['Equity, disability and respectful coordination',[
'Equality means providing the same thing; equity asks whether differences in barriers require different supports for meaningful opportunity. Giving every family the same printed instructions may be equal, but it does not meet the needs of a person who requires a different language, large print or a demonstration. Accessibility includes physical space, communication, sensory needs and the design of procedures. Disability should not be reduced to an inability located entirely within an individual; an inaccessible environment can create or magnify participation barriers.',
'Coordination works best when the person and family help define the goal. Ask what matters: attending a community activity, communicating with classmates, maintaining employment or managing a household task. Then identify who can address each barrier. A clinician may assess function, a school may adjust instruction and a community organization may assist with transportation. Sharing information requires appropriate consent and privacy protections; “working together” does not authorize unrestricted disclosure. Explain what will be shared, why it is needed and who will receive it.',
'Avoid a rescue narrative in which professionals decide everything for a passive family. A caregiver may already have effective strategies that are invisible during a clinic visit. Offer choices, acknowledge constraints and check whether the proposed plan is realistic. When a recommendation cannot be followed, investigate the obstacle before repeating it more forcefully. Feasibility is part of the intervention, not an optional courtesy.'] ],
['Evaluate the plan, not just the referral',[
'Return to the missed evaluation. The immediate barrier is a mismatch between transport and appointment hours. Possible actions include a different appointment time, an accessible location or a transport resource, depending on what is available and appropriate. Sending another reminder alone will not address this mechanism. Ask the family which option could work, confirm the arrangements and establish a follow-up point. A plan that exists only on paper has not yet improved access.',
'Outcomes should reflect the intended goal. Counting referrals measures activity, while successful attendance measures an intermediate result. Participation, communication or function may be the longer-term outcome. Track unintended burdens as well: a plan might improve clinic attendance while requiring a caregiver to miss essential income. Developmental supports often involve tradeoffs, so success requires more than one number. Compare observations over time and revise the plan when the barrier or goal changes.',
sort('Match the level of support',['Broad population support','Individual access adjustment','Outcome measurement'],[['Accessible playground design for a community',0],['Arrange an interpreter for a scheduled evaluation',1],['Track whether the child can participate in the chosen activity',2],['Public information on developmental monitoring',0],['Move one appointment to a workable time',1],['Ask whether the family completed the agreed next step',2]]),
flip('Trace the pathway',[['A resource exists. Is access solved?','No. Location, cost, schedule, language and accessibility can still prevent use.'],['A family misses a visit. What can you infer?','Only that the visit was missed; investigate barriers before inferring motivation.'],['What distinguishes a referral from an outcome?','A referral initiates a pathway; an outcome shows whether the intended change occurred.'],['What does equity add?','Attention to different barriers so people have meaningful opportunities.']]),
'For written responses, describe one mechanism, one feasible action and one way to evaluate it. This makes the answer specific enough to be useful without claiming that a single program can remove all developmental risk. Include the person’s preferences and avoid inventing eligibility rules.'] ]],
takeaways:['Resources influence development through specific daily mechanisms.','Availability and accessibility are different.','Evaluate participation and function, not merely the number of referrals.'],terms:[['Protective factor','A condition that supports adaptation or reduces risk.'],['Equity','Addressing differing barriers to meaningful opportunity.']],
mcqs:[q('A clinic is free but unreachable by public transport. What remains unresolved?','Practical access','The existence of the clinic','Whether development is lifelong','Whether all families have identical needs','A service can exist without being practically accessible.'),q('Which is an upstream influence?','Housing instability','One observed yawn','A single quiz score','A momentary smile','Housing conditions can shape repeated daily experiences.'),q('Which statement correctly describes risk?','A risk factor changes probability without determining an individual outcome','A risk factor proves a diagnosis','Risk always produces the same result','Protective factors eliminate all risk','Risk is probabilistic, not destiny.'),q('Which response best addresses missed visits caused by work schedules?','Discuss appointment options that fit the family’s constraints','Send increasingly judgmental reminders','Assume the caregiver dislikes the child','Stop offering assessment','The action should address the identified mechanism.'),q('Which is a meaningful longer-term outcome?','Improved participation in the activity the person chose','Number of brochures printed','Number of referral forms sent','Number of staff meetings held','Participation measures the goal rather than only administrative activity.'),q('Which action demonstrates equity?','Provide communication formats suited to different access needs','Require one format for everyone regardless of need','Exclude people who need assistance','Assume identical barriers','Equity responds to different barriers.'),q('What should occur before sharing information across services?','Confirm the appropriate consent and privacy requirements','Send the entire record to everyone','Assume a referral removes confidentiality','Post the case publicly','Coordination still requires privacy protections.'),q('What is the best description of an early-intervention referral?','A step toward assessment and possible support','Proof of permanent disability','A guarantee of one specific service','A replacement for the family’s participation','Referral begins a process; it does not establish the final determination.')],
frq:{prompt:'Design an access plan for a family that cannot reach a developmental clinic during its current hours. Explain the mechanism, one individualized adjustment and how you would evaluate progress.',rubric:[['Explain the access mechanism',['transport','schedule','barrier']],['Include a feasible shared plan',['family','appointment','option']],['Measure meaningful progress',['attendance','participation','follow']]],model:'The barrier is the interaction between transport hours and clinic scheduling, not demonstrated lack of motivation. I would ask which appointment times and locations are workable, then help coordinate an available option with the family’s agreement. Follow-up would confirm whether the visit occurred and whether the recommended support improved the child’s participation. I would also ask about new burdens, such as missed wages, and revise the arrangement if it is not sustainable.'},sources:[HOSA,CDC,MILE]
});

lessons.push({
title:'Theories of development: compare explanations, not just names',
hook:'A preschooler says a tall narrow cup contains more juice than a short wide cup, even after watching the same juice poured between them. A theory helps explain the response, but a single answer does not define the child’s total intelligence.',
objectives:['Compare cognitive, psychosocial, learning and ecological explanations.','Apply Piaget, Erikson, Vygotsky and learning principles to cases.','Recognize limitations of stage models and historical theories.','Distinguish a theory-based prediction from a diagnostic conclusion.'],
sections:[
['Piaget and changes in reasoning',[
'Piaget described children as active constructors of knowledge. A **schema** is an organized way of understanding an experience. **Assimilation** incorporates an experience into an existing schema, while **accommodation** changes the schema when the old explanation does not fit. A child who calls every four-legged animal a dog is assimilating new animals into a familiar category. Learning to distinguish cats from dogs requires accommodation. These terms describe the organization of understanding, not simply memorizing a new word.',
'The sensorimotor period emphasizes learning through action and sensation, including developing understanding that objects continue to exist when hidden. Preoperational thinking includes symbolic play and language but can be strongly influenced by appearances. In concrete operational reasoning, children become more able to coordinate multiple features, understand conservation and reverse a mental operation when the problem concerns concrete situations. Formal operational reasoning involves possibilities, abstract relationships and systematic hypothesis testing. The familiar age ranges are approximate teaching conventions; task demands, experience and methods of questioning affect performance.',
'The cup example illustrates **centration**, attention to one striking feature such as height. Conservation requires understanding that the quantity remains the same despite a change in appearance. Avoid the mistake of equating egocentrism with selfishness: in this theory it concerns difficulty coordinating another perspective. Also avoid assuming an older person always uses abstract reasoning. People may reason more effectively with familiar content, visual support or additional time.'] ],
['Erikson and psychosocial tasks',[
'Erikson emphasized tensions that arise as people negotiate relationships and social expectations. His model includes trust versus mistrust, autonomy versus shame and doubt, initiative versus guilt, industry versus inferiority, identity versus role confusion, intimacy versus isolation, generativity versus stagnation and integrity versus despair. Each pair is a useful lens, not a pass-or-fail test that permanently determines personality. Earlier concerns can reappear when circumstances change, and later experiences can support new adaptation.',
'An infant’s reliable care can support trust. A toddler’s safe choices can support autonomy. A young child can practice initiative through play, while a school-age child can develop industry by completing meaningful tasks with feedback. Adolescence often brings exploration of identity, values and belonging. Adult intimacy concerns reciprocal closeness and does not require marriage. Generativity includes contributing to future generations through mentoring, work, care or community participation; it is not limited to parenthood. Integrity involves making sense of one’s life, including losses and imperfections, rather than insisting that everything was ideal.',
'These concepts can guide support. A hospitalized school-age child may value helping organize their own supplies because it restores competence. An older adult may value telling a life story because it supports meaning and continuity. However, the same activity will not fit everyone. Ask what the person wants and avoid making a stage label into an excuse to ignore cultural context or individual priorities.'],],
['Learning, relationships and supported performance',[
'Learning theories explain how experience changes behavior. Classical conditioning involves learning associations between stimuli: a child may feel anxious when entering a room associated with a painful procedure. Operant conditioning concerns consequences that influence the future likelihood of behavior. Reinforcement increases behavior; punishment decreases it. Positive means adding something, and negative means removing something. Negative reinforcement is therefore not punishment. If fastening a seat belt stops an unpleasant alarm and increases fastening, removal of the alarm reinforces the behavior.',
'Social learning emphasizes observation, modeling and expectations. A child may learn a calming strategy by watching a trusted adult use it. Vygotsky emphasized social interaction and the role of cultural tools, especially language. The **zone of proximal development** describes tasks a learner cannot yet manage independently but can perform with appropriate support. **Scaffolding** is temporary assistance adjusted as competence grows. Demonstrating the first step, asking a guiding question and gradually withdrawing help can support learning without taking over the task.',
'Attachment theory addresses the use of close caregivers as sources of security and support. Attachment is not identical to affection, temperament or a single separation response. Patterns are interpreted from relationships over time and within assessment context. A brief clinic observation should not be used to label a caregiver or child. Reliable relationships can support exploration, but development also reflects wider conditions and opportunities.'] ],
['Using theories critically',[
'Historical psychodynamic approaches drew attention to early experience and unconscious processes. Freud’s psychosexual stages remain relevant to the history of developmental thought, but many specific claims are difficult to test and should not be presented as established explanations for individual behavior. Kohlberg’s model focuses on the reasoning used to justify moral decisions, ranging from consequences and rules toward broader principles. It does not simply score whether someone chose the “right” action, and moral reasoning is not identical to actual behavior.',
'Bronfenbrenner’s ecological approach directs attention to interacting settings and historical change. It complements rather than replaces a cognitive or learning explanation. Consider a child struggling with homework: Piaget may direct attention to the reasoning demands, Vygotsky to the support available, learning theory to practice and feedback, and ecological theory to household routines and school expectations. A stronger answer identifies which part of the case each theory explains and what it leaves unexplained.',
match('Match the explanation to the concept',[['Changing a category after encountering contradictory evidence','Accommodation'],['Solving a task with temporary prompts','Scaffolding'],['Increasing behavior by removing an unpleasant stimulus','Negative reinforcement'],['Coordinating several concrete features of a problem','Concrete operational reasoning'],['Contributing through mentoring or community work','Generativity'],['Considering relationships among home, school and policy','Ecological reasoning']]),
sort('Which theory best fits this emphasis?',['Cognitive construction','Psychosocial relationships','Learning through consequences'],[['Revising a schema',0],['Identity and belonging',1],['Reinforcement increases responding',2],['Conservation despite changed appearance',0],['Integrity while reviewing a life',1],['A consequence reduces a behavior',2]]),
'On the exam, focus on the mechanism in the question. A reward is not automatically reinforcement unless behavior increases. A stage name does not establish a diagnosis. When several theories could apply, choose the one that most directly explains the described process. In an FRQ, state the theory, connect a specific detail and acknowledge a limit instead of listing theorists without application.'] ]],
takeaways:['Theories answer different questions and can complement one another.','Stage ranges summarize broad patterns, not fixed deadlines.','Reinforcement increases behavior; negative means removing a stimulus.'],terms:[['Accommodation','Changing a schema to incorporate information that does not fit.'],['Scaffolding','Temporary support adjusted to a learner’s developing independence.']],
mcqs:[q('A child changes the category “dog” after learning how cats differ. Which process is emphasized?','Accommodation','Negative punishment','Generativity','Classical extinction','The schema changes rather than simply absorbing another example.'),q('A child focuses only on a cup’s height. Which Piagetian concept fits?','Centration','Integrity','Scaffolding','Generativity','Centration emphasizes one feature while neglecting others.'),q('Which action supports autonomy?','Offer two safe choices the child can manage','Complete every possible task for the child','Shame the child for needing practice','Remove every boundary','Safe choices support developing independence.'),q('An alarm stops after a seat belt is fastened and fastening increases. What is this?','Negative reinforcement','Positive punishment','Negative punishment','Accommodation','Removing an unpleasant stimulus increases the behavior.'),q('Which example best illustrates scaffolding?','Provide a prompt and reduce assistance as the learner improves','Give the final answer every time','Require unsupported success from the start','Ignore the learner’s current skill','Scaffolding is temporary, calibrated support.'),q('Which statement about generativity is accurate?','Mentoring and community contribution can express it','It occurs only in biological parents','It requires a particular occupation','It means avoiding younger generations','Generativity includes many forms of contribution.'),q('What does Kohlberg’s approach primarily examine?','Reasoning used to justify a moral decision','A person’s measured height','A fixed grief sequence','Only the final action without explanation','The justification is central to the moral-reasoning framework.'),q('What is the strongest use of developmental theories in a case?','Connect each relevant mechanism to evidence and recognize limits','Assign one stage and stop assessing','Treat historical claims as unquestionable facts','Assume every theory predicts the same thing','Theory supports interpretation but does not replace evidence or context.')],
frq:{prompt:'A child can solve a sorting task with prompts but not alone and becomes discouraged after repeated public criticism. Apply two developmental theories and propose a supportive change.',rubric:[['Explain supported learning',['scaffold','support','zone']],['Connect psychosocial experience',['industry','competence','criticism']],['Propose a specific adjustment',['prompt','feedback','gradual']]],model:'Vygotsky’s approach suggests that the task lies within the child’s supported learning range: prompts allow success that is not yet independent. I would retain useful cues and gradually reduce them. Erikson’s industry-versus-inferiority lens highlights how repeated public criticism may undermine a sense of competence. Specific private feedback and achievable practice can support mastery. These theories explain different aspects of the case, but additional observation is needed before assuming the child’s difficulty has one cause.'},sources:[HOSA,PSY,CDC]
});

lessons.push({
title:'Prenatal development: timing, systems and protective care',
hook:'A student reads that an exposure occurred “at six weeks” but does not know whether the author means weeks since fertilization or clinical gestational age. Before interpreting developmental timing, establish the clock being used.',
objectives:['Describe germinal, embryonic and fetal development.','Explain the placenta and fetal circulation at a conceptual level.','Evaluate exposure risk using timing, dose and context.','Connect preconception and prenatal care with developmental protection.'],
sections:[
['Establish the timeline',[
'Prenatal development begins with fertilization, when genetic material from sperm and egg combines in a zygote. Clinical **gestational age** is commonly dated from the first day of the last menstrual period, so it is approximately two weeks ahead of age measured from fertilization in a typical cycle. Cycle variation and clinical assessment can affect dating. When comparing a developmental description with an appointment record, identify which convention is used before adding or subtracting weeks. Confusing the clocks can produce an incorrect interpretation of when an exposure occurred.',
'In the early germinal period, cell divisions increase the number of cells as the developing structure moves toward the uterus. A blastocyst forms and implantation establishes a relationship with the uterine lining. The embryonic period, conventionally weeks three through eight after fertilization, includes extensive organization of major body structures. The fetal period begins around week nine after fertilization and involves growth, differentiation and continuing functional maturation. These labels describe dominant processes; development is continuous, and a system that begins early continues changing later.',
'A timeline should not imply that the fetus becomes unaffected by the environment after organs first appear. Brain development, growth and physiological maturation continue throughout pregnancy. Nor does an exposure at a particular time prove a specific outcome. The timeline identifies a question to investigate: what was developing, what exposure occurred and what reliable clinical information is available?'] ],
['The placenta and the developing body',[
'The placenta supports exchange between maternal and fetal systems. Oxygen and nutrients move toward the fetus while carbon dioxide and metabolic wastes move away. Maternal and fetal blood are normally separated by placental tissues rather than simply mixing in one shared pool. The placenta also produces hormones that support pregnancy. It is not an absolute filter: some substances and infectious agents can cross or affect placental function. Consequently, “the placenta protects the fetus” is too vague to establish the safety of a particular exposure.',
'The umbilical vein carries relatively oxygen-rich blood from the placenta toward the fetus; umbilical arteries carry blood back toward the placenta. This is a useful reminder that arteries and veins are named by direction relative to the heart, not by oxygen content. Fetal circulation includes pathways that direct blood around organs whose functions differ before birth. The foramen ovale allows flow between the atria, the ductus arteriosus connects pulmonary and systemic outflow, and the ductus venosus allows some blood to bypass hepatic circulation. These pathways change as breathing and independent circulation begin after birth.',
'The developing nervous system, heart and other organs emerge through coordinated cell division, migration and differentiation. A cell’s location and interactions matter alongside its genetic information. For an introductory developmental answer, explain the sequence and purpose rather than pretending that naming three germ layers alone explains all organ formation. The clinical significance is that development involves many interacting processes with different sensitive periods.'] ],
['Exposure risk is a question of mechanism',[
'A **teratogen** is an agent capable of disrupting prenatal development under relevant exposure conditions. Risk depends on the agent, dose, duration, timing and susceptibility, including genetic and health factors. A single word such as “chemical” cannot establish risk. The same general category can contain substances with very different effects. Organ formation is especially sensitive to some structural disruptions, while later exposures may affect growth or function. An absence of an obvious birth difference does not establish that every developmental function is unaffected.',
'Alcohol exposure during pregnancy can cause fetal alcohol spectrum disorders; there is no established safe amount or timing for alcohol use during pregnancy. Tobacco smoke and other harmful exposures also warrant prevention and support. Discussions should be factual and nonjudgmental so that people can disclose concerns and receive help. If someone reports an exposure, the appropriate response is to gather accurate details and refer to qualified care, not to declare inevitable harm or promise that everything is safe.',
'Medication decisions require balancing the effects of the medicine with the risks of an untreated condition. A learner should never advise abruptly stopping an essential prescribed treatment because pregnancy is possible. The person should contact the prescribing and prenatal clinicians for an individualized review. Include supplements and nonprescription products in that review. “Natural” is a description of origin, not proof of prenatal safety.'] ],
['Protective care and a realistic case analysis',[
'Preconception care can identify health conditions, medication issues and vaccination needs before pregnancy. Folic acid is important because neural tube development occurs early; public-health guidance commonly recommends 400 micrograms daily for people who could become pregnant, with individualized recommendations for some circumstances. Prenatal care monitors maternal and fetal health, addresses questions and helps identify complications. Screening estimates risk or identifies a reason for further evaluation; diagnostic testing answers a more specific question. These are different purposes and should be explained clearly.',
'Access and emotional support belong in the prenatal picture. Work conditions, transport, food access, safety and mental health can affect whether a care plan is feasible. A clinician can make a technically correct recommendation that remains unusable without practical support. Respectful counseling asks about constraints and avoids equating a missed visit with disregard for the pregnancy. Decisions about screening and care should include informed discussion of benefits, limitations and the person’s preferences.',
{t:'w',kind:'order',title:'Order the broad prenatal sequence',steps:['Fertilization forms a zygote','Early cell divisions and blastocyst formation','Implantation in the uterine lining','Embryonic organization of major body structures','Fetal growth and continuing functional maturation']},
match('Connect structure and function',[['Placenta','Exchange and hormonal support at the maternal–fetal interface'],['Umbilical vein','Carries blood from placenta toward fetus'],['Umbilical arteries','Carry blood from fetus toward placenta'],['Gestational age','Clinical dating convention commonly beginning with the last menstrual period'],['Screening','Estimates risk or identifies a need for further evaluation']]),
'For the opening case, first clarify the date convention. Then document the substance, amount, timing and relevant health history. Avoid converting a broad sensitive-period diagram into an individual prognosis. The developmental lesson is how to organize the questions and seek appropriate interpretation, not how to diagnose fetal injury from a short scenario.'] ]],
takeaways:['Gestational and post-fertilization ages use different starting points.','The placenta supports exchange but is not an absolute barrier.','Exposure timing informs assessment without determining an individual outcome.'],terms:[['Teratogen','An agent capable of disrupting prenatal development under relevant conditions.'],['Implantation','Attachment and embedding of the early developing structure in the uterine lining.']],
mcqs:[q('Why can “six weeks” be ambiguous?','Gestational and post-fertilization ages use different starting points','All pregnancies develop at identical rates','Weeks cannot describe development','The fetal period begins at fertilization','The dating convention must be established.'),q('Which period emphasizes organization of major body structures?','Embryonic','Late adulthood','Postnatal infancy only','Only the final week before birth','Organogenesis is a defining emphasis of the embryonic period.'),q('Which statement about the placenta is correct?','It supports exchange but does not block every harmful substance','It guarantees protection from all medications','Maternal and fetal blood always mix freely','It has no hormonal role','Placental functions include exchange and hormone production without an absolute barrier.'),q('Which vessel carries blood from placenta toward fetus?','Umbilical vein','Umbilical artery','Pulmonary artery after birth','A maternal coronary artery','The umbilical vein returns blood from the placenta to the fetus.'),q('Which combination best informs exposure assessment?','Agent, dose, timing, duration and susceptibility','Only whether the product is natural','Only the brand name','Only the person’s age','Risk depends on the exposure and developmental context.'),q('What should a pregnant person do about an essential prescribed medication concern?','Discuss an individualized review with the treating clinician','Stop it immediately without advice','Assume every medication is safe','Double the dose to compensate','Both medication effects and untreated illness must be considered.'),q('What is the main distinction between screening and diagnosis?','Screening identifies risk or need for evaluation; diagnosis addresses a specific determination','Screening always proves a condition','Diagnosis never uses evidence','The terms mean exactly the same thing','The purpose and certainty of the procedures differ.'),q('Why does preconception folic acid matter?','Neural tube development occurs early','It eliminates every congenital condition','It replaces all prenatal care','It determines the infant’s temperament','Early developmental timing makes preconception protection important.')],
frq:{prompt:'A person reports an early-pregnancy exposure and asks whether harm is certain. Explain how to organize the assessment without offering an unsupported prognosis.',rubric:[['Clarify timing',['gestational','fertilization','timing']],['Characterize the exposure',['dose','duration','substance']],['Explain uncertainty and appropriate care',['risk','clinician','assessment']]],model:'I would first clarify whether the reported week is gestational age or time since fertilization. I would record the actual substance, amount, duration and relevant health information. Developmental timing helps identify which processes may be sensitive, but it cannot establish a certain outcome. The person should receive a qualified clinical assessment, including review of any medications rather than stopping them independently. I would communicate uncertainty clearly and support timely prenatal care without blame.'},sources:[HOSA,PRE,['OpenStax: Embryonic development','https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology/pages/28-2-embryonic-development']]
});

lessons.push({
title:'Infancy and toddlerhood: regulation, attachment and exploration',
hook:'A toddler insists on holding a spoon, spills food and cries when an adult takes over. The scene combines motor practice, autonomy, frustration and the need for support. Understanding the domains helps the caregiver respond to the whole situation.',
objectives:['Connect early motor, cognitive, language and social-emotional development.','Distinguish monitoring from screening and evaluation.','Explain responsive care and emerging self-regulation.','Apply safety principles while supporting exploration.'],
sections:[
['Patterns of physical and cognitive change',[
'Early development involves rapid changes in movement, perception and ways of exploring. Broad patterns often progress from control of the head and trunk toward more coordinated limb movements, and from larger movements toward more precise manipulation. These descriptions summarize tendencies rather than a strict sequence that every child follows identically. Infants vary in the ways they move and practice, and some behaviors are not required stepping stones for every child. Compare observations with appropriate developmental resources and the child’s history instead of using one remembered age as a verdict.',
'Gross motor skills involve large muscle groups and posture; fine motor skills involve more precise actions such as grasping and manipulating objects. Development in these areas opens new cognitive opportunities. Sitting with support changes what an infant can see and reach. Moving independently allows exploration but also increases exposure to hazards. A child repeatedly dropping a toy may be investigating cause and effect rather than attempting to annoy an adult. The same action can be motor practice, an experiment and an invitation to social interaction.',
'Object permanence develops as the child increasingly understands that a person or object continues to exist when out of sight. Exploration also depends on attention, memory and the ability to coordinate actions. A task can be difficult because it requires several abilities at once. When observing a child, make the task accessible and note what assistance was provided so that supported performance is not confused with independent performance.'] ],
['Communication and relationships',[
'Communication begins before spoken words. Looking, gestures, vocalizations and turn-taking can all serve communicative purposes. Receptive language concerns understanding; expressive language concerns conveying meaning. A child may understand more than they can say, but this general pattern does not justify ignoring a caregiver’s concern. Ask about communication across settings and, for multilingual families, across the languages used. Consider hearing, opportunities for interaction and other developmental information when concerns arise.',
'Responsive care means noticing signals, interpreting them thoughtfully and responding in a way that supports the child’s needs. The caregiver will not interpret every signal correctly, and healthy interaction does not require perfect attunement. Repairing a mismatch can itself support the relationship: the adult notices that the child is overwhelmed, reduces stimulation and tries again. Attachment-related security can provide a base for exploration and a source of comfort during distress. A single episode of crying at separation is not sufficient to classify an attachment pattern.',
'Temperament describes individual tendencies in reactivity and regulation. Some children approach novelty quickly; others need time. A **goodness of fit** approach adjusts expectations and support to the child while helping them develop skills. It avoids labeling a cautious child as defective or an active child as bad. Predictable routines and patient transitions may be especially helpful when the child is easily overwhelmed.'] ],
['Autonomy and co-regulation',[
'Toddlers increasingly want to act independently while still needing substantial help. This mismatch can produce frustration. **Co-regulation** occurs when an adult helps manage emotion and behavior through calm presence, structure and responsive support. Self-regulation develops gradually through repeated practice; it is not a switch that turns on at a birthday. An adult can acknowledge the feeling while holding a limit: “You wanted to keep playing. It is time to leave. You may hold my hand or ride in the stroller.”',
'In the spoon example, taking over immediately may end the mess but remove an opportunity for practice. A better response may be to use an appropriate utensil, offer a manageable amount of food and provide help when needed. The adult still addresses choking and other safety concerns. Supporting autonomy does not mean allowing every choice, and setting limits does not require humiliation. Distinguish the child’s feeling from the unsafe action: frustration is acceptable, but hitting another person requires a calm, consistent boundary.',
'Play supports problem solving, imitation, communication and relationships. Activities should match the child’s abilities and safety needs rather than aim to accelerate every milestone. Shared attention to a book, naming objects during a routine and giving time for a response are examples of everyday learning opportunities. An expensive product is not required to create a responsive interaction. The quality and fit of the experience matter more than marketing claims.'] ],
['Monitoring, concerns and safe environments',[
'Developmental monitoring follows a child’s changes over time through everyday observation and discussion. Screening uses a standardized tool to examine development more systematically. A formal evaluation investigates a concern in greater depth. CDC milestone materials can support monitoring but do not replace validated screening tools. A missed milestone, loss of a previously established skill or caregiver concern deserves discussion with the child’s health professional. Variation should not become a reason to postpone appropriate assessment.',
'Safety needs change as mobility and curiosity increase. Secure hazardous substances, address choking risks and supervise activities appropriate to the child’s capabilities. For infant sleep, authoritative guidance recommends beginning each sleep on the back and using an appropriate safe sleep environment; awake supervised tummy time serves a different purpose. Do not confuse a developmental activity with a sleeping position. Caregiver education should include demonstration and questions, especially when written instructions are difficult to use.',
sort('Identify the developmental domain emphasized',['Motor','Communication','Social-emotional','Cognitive'],[['Uses a pincer-like grasp to pick up an appropriate object',0],['Points to request help',1],['Seeks comfort from a familiar caregiver',2],['Searches for a hidden toy',3],['Uses words to convey a request',1],['Practices balancing while standing',0]]),
flip('Choose the next observation',[['A toddler spills while self-feeding.','Observe motor control, utensil fit, fatigue and the level of help before deciding the child should not try.'],['A child becomes distressed during transition.','Consider predictability, sensory demands and co-regulation rather than assuming deliberate misconduct.'],['A caregiver reports loss of a skill.','Take the report seriously and support timely professional assessment.'],['A milestone checklist identifies concern.','Use it to discuss next steps; it is not a diagnosis or a substitute for standardized screening.']]),
'A good developmental response combines opportunity with protection. The goal is neither to prevent all frustration nor to leave the child unsupported. Identify the skill being practiced, remove unnecessary barriers and provide enough assistance for safe learning. Record what changes over time so that the support can evolve with the child.'] ]],
takeaways:['Motor, communication and emotional skills develop together.','Co-regulation supports emerging independence.','Monitoring tools guide discussion but do not diagnose.'],terms:[['Co-regulation','Support from another person that helps manage emotion and behavior.'],['Receptive language','Understanding communicated meaning.']],
mcqs:[q('Which example emphasizes fine motor development?','Manipulating a small age-appropriate object with the fingers','Running across a field','Balancing the trunk while standing','Jumping with both feet','Fine motor skills involve precise manipulation.'),q('What is receptive language?','Understanding communicated meaning','Only speaking clearly','Only writing words','Any large-muscle movement','Receptive and expressive communication describe different functions.'),q('What does searching for a hidden toy suggest?','Developing object permanence','A universal diagnosis','Completed formal operational thought','Lack of social interest','The child is showing understanding that the object continues to exist.'),q('Which response best supports toddler autonomy?','Provide safe choices and manageable assistance','Remove all limits','Shame every mistake','Perform every task before the child tries','Autonomy grows through supported practice within safety limits.'),q('What is co-regulation?','An adult helping a child manage emotion and behavior','Punishing every emotion','Expecting immediate independent control','Ignoring distress entirely','Calm, responsive support helps build later regulation.'),q('Which statement about a CDC milestone checklist is correct?','It supports monitoring but does not replace validated screening','It diagnoses every developmental condition','It guarantees no concern if one item is met','It replaces caregiver observations','Monitoring and screening have different roles.'),q('What warrants timely professional discussion?','Loss of a previously established skill','Every harmless preference','Any bilingual exposure','Any variation in play interests','Regression or caregiver concern should be assessed rather than dismissed.'),q('Which distinction is important for infant safety?','Awake supervised tummy time differs from sleep positioning','Tummy time means placing infants prone for sleep','Developmental practice removes the need for supervision','One safe environment fits every activity','Activities and sleep have different safety requirements.')],
frq:{prompt:'A toddler wants to feed independently, becomes upset when corrected and has a caregiver concerned about language. Explain a response that supports autonomy while taking the communication concern seriously.',rubric:[['Support safe motor practice',['utensil','practice','safe']],['Explain co-regulation',['calm','emotion','support']],['Address communication assessment',['language','hearing','screen']]],model:'I would make self-feeding manageable with a suitable utensil and safe food while offering help rather than immediately taking over. A calm acknowledgment of frustration and consistent limits support co-regulation. Separately, I would ask about understanding, gestures and expression across familiar settings and languages, and help the caregiver discuss the concern with the child’s clinician. Milestone monitoring can organize observations, but a standardized screen or evaluation may be needed; autonomy practice does not resolve every communication concern.'},sources:[HOSA,CDC,MILE,['NICHD: Back sleeping and infant safety','https://safetosleep.nichd.nih.gov/reduce-risk/back-sleeping']]
});

lessons.push({
title:'Childhood: play, competence, learning and relationships',
hook:'A child can explain a science idea using blocks but struggles to explain it in writing. Another child finishes worksheets quickly yet cannot resolve a playground disagreement. Childhood development is a profile of skills, not a single score.',
objectives:['Compare early-childhood and school-age learning opportunities.','Connect play with language, executive skills and social understanding.','Distinguish specific performance barriers from global judgments.','Design feedback that supports competence and inclusion.'],
sections:[
['Bodies, practice and a changing environment',[
'During childhood, growth and coordination support increasingly complex movement. Running, climbing, drawing, dressing and manipulating tools involve different combinations of strength, balance, planning and practice. A child who is skilled in one activity may still be a beginner in another. Physical size does not directly measure coordination, and neither should be used as a measure of character. Opportunities to practice are affected by accessible space, equipment, instruction and the child’s interests. A developmental description should account for these opportunities rather than assuming all children have experienced the same tasks.',
'Health habits are part of the learning environment. Sleep, nutrition, movement and management of illness can influence energy and attention. They do not provide a simple explanation for every school difficulty, but they belong in a broad assessment. A tired child may appear uninterested during a difficult task. A child who cannot see the board may stop participating. Before assigning a motivation label, examine whether the task and environment are accessible and whether a health concern requires attention.',
'Increasing independence creates new responsibilities without eliminating the need for supervision. Children can learn routines for crossing a street, using equipment or managing school materials, but instruction should match actual skill and environmental demands. An adult should not infer readiness from age alone. Ask the child to demonstrate understanding in a safe context, provide feedback and revisit the skill as conditions change.'] ],
['Play and the development of thinking',[
'Play can involve exploration, pretend scenarios, construction, rules and collaboration. Pretend play allows children to use symbols and practice perspectives: a block can stand for a telephone, and a child can take the role of a shopkeeper. Rule-based games require remembering constraints, waiting and adjusting actions. These activities can exercise executive skills such as working memory, inhibitory control and cognitive flexibility. The skills develop gradually and are sensitive to task complexity, fatigue and support.',
'Piaget’s preoperational and concrete operational descriptions help organize common changes in reasoning. A younger child may focus on appearance or find another viewpoint difficult to coordinate. A school-age child may more successfully compare several features, classify objects and understand reversibility in concrete situations. However, a poorly worded question can hide what the child understands. Use familiar objects and ask the child to explain the reasoning. A correct answer obtained by guessing provides different evidence from an explanation that transfers to a new example.',
'Language expands through conversation, reading, storytelling and participation. Narrative skill involves organizing events, explaining motives and adapting information for a listener. Academic language adds unfamiliar vocabulary and forms of explanation. A child may communicate effectively in everyday life while needing support with a scientific explanation or a written response. This is a reason to teach the task explicitly, not evidence that the child has no knowledge. Separate the conceptual demand from the reading, writing or motor demands when investigating difficulty.'] ],
['Competence, peers and emotional understanding',[
'School-age children encounter more explicit comparisons and evaluations. Erikson’s industry-versus-inferiority lens highlights the value of meaningful accomplishment and constructive feedback. Praise that identifies an effective strategy helps a child understand what can be repeated: “You checked the labels before sorting” is more informative than “You are smart.” Feedback should also make the next step manageable. Constant comparison with classmates can obscure individual progress and discourage a child whose strengths are not represented by the activity.',
'Peer relationships offer practice in cooperation, negotiation, perspective taking and managing disagreement. Conflict is not always bullying. Bullying involves a harmful pattern with a power imbalance, and it requires appropriate adult response rather than simply telling the child to become tougher. Exclusion and discrimination can undermine belonging and participation. Adults should gather specific information, support safety and avoid making the targeted child solely responsible for fixing the situation.',
'Emotional understanding includes recognizing feelings, naming them and selecting an effective response. A child may identify anger but still need help delaying an action. Teach a strategy in a calm moment, model it and practice it in manageable situations. Expecting a new skill to appear automatically during a highly upsetting event is unrealistic. The adult can maintain a boundary while helping the child recover and reflect afterward. This supports accountability without humiliation.'] ],
['A fair learning and support plan',[
'Return to the child who explains with blocks but struggles in writing. The difference suggests that conceptual understanding and written expression should be assessed separately. Possible contributors include handwriting demands, language organization, working memory, unfamiliar vocabulary or a mismatch between instructions and prior teaching. It does not establish a diagnosis. Ask for an oral explanation, examine a short supported writing sample and gather observations from different tasks. If concerns persist, use the appropriate educational or clinical assessment pathway.',
'Scaffolding can preserve the learning goal while reducing an unnecessary barrier. A visual organizer may help structure an explanation; a demonstration may clarify an unfamiliar procedure. Reduce support as the learner becomes more independent, but do not remove an accessibility accommodation merely to make the task harder. Accommodation changes access, while a modification may change the learning expectation. The purpose should be clear to the learner and team so that performance is interpreted fairly.',
match('Connect the classroom observation to a next step',[['Understands orally but struggles in writing','Separate conceptual knowledge from expression demands'],['Forgets several instructions at once','Try shorter steps and a visible sequence'],['Becomes distressed after repeated exclusion','Investigate safety, belonging and the peer context'],['Completes a task only with prompts','Document supported performance and gradually adjust help'],['Shows an unexplained change across several activities','Gather broader information rather than assigning a motivation label']]),
sort('Classify the feedback',['Specific and useful','Global or shaming'],[['You checked your work; now compare the second step.',0],['You are bad at everything.',1],['Let us practice one part before adding the next.',0],['Everyone else can do this, so stop trying excuses.',1],['Your example explains the idea; add a reason.',0]]),
'A strong written case response identifies the observed discrepancy, offers plausible explanations and proposes evidence that could distinguish them. Include the child’s perspective and a plan for reviewing progress. Developmental support is an iterative process: teach, observe, adjust and reassess. A single test score or classroom incident is one piece of the picture rather than the whole child.'] ]],
takeaways:['Childhood skills develop unevenly across domains and tasks.','Specific feedback supports competence better than global labels.','Investigate task demands, access and relationships before inferring motivation.'],terms:[['Executive skills','Processes used to hold information, manage impulses and shift strategies.'],['Scaffolding','Calibrated assistance that supports developing independent performance.']],
mcqs:[q('A child explains with objects but not in writing. What should be examined first?','Differences between conceptual and expression demands','Whether the child is universally incapable','Only the child’s height','Whether all teaching should stop','Different tasks can reveal different abilities and barriers.'),q('Which feedback is most useful?','You compared both features; now explain your reason','You are always the best','You never understand anything','Everyone else is faster','Specific feedback identifies a strategy and a next step.'),q('Which activity especially practices inhibitory control?','Waiting for a turn in a rule-based game','Memorizing height measurements','Avoiding all interaction','Being given every answer','Waiting requires regulating an immediate response.'),q('Which statement about concrete operational reasoning is most accurate?','Reasoning often improves when relationships are concrete and familiar','All abstract reasoning is automatically mastered','Performance is identical on every task','Age alone proves competence','Task content and support affect observed reasoning.'),q('What distinguishes bullying from an ordinary disagreement?','A harmful pattern involving a power imbalance','Any difference of opinion','Any moment of frustration','Any solitary play','The pattern and power relationship matter for interpretation and response.'),q('Which is an appropriate response to unexplained withdrawal?','Gather information about health, tasks and peer experiences','Assume laziness','Ignore all caregiver reports','Punish the child for being quiet','Multiple domains and contexts may explain a change.'),q('Why practice emotion-management strategies during calm periods?','The child can learn the strategy before needing it under stress','Emotions should never be discussed','Practice guarantees no future distress','Calm practice eliminates all limits','Learning a strategy and using it under stress are related but different demands.'),q('Which plan best supports industry?','Provide meaningful tasks, achievable steps and constructive feedback','Compare the child unfavorably with every peer','Do all work for the child','Avoid all opportunities to practice','Competence grows through supported mastery.')],
frq:{prompt:'A school-age child shows strong oral reasoning but weak written work and is increasingly excluded by peers. Develop a plan that addresses learning and social participation without assuming a single cause.',rubric:[['Separate task demands',['oral','writing','demand']],['Address peer context',['peer','exclusion','safety']],['Use supported practice and review',['support','feedback','progress']]],model:'I would compare oral explanations with supported written samples to identify whether language organization, handwriting, working memory or instructions create barriers. This does not by itself establish a diagnosis. I would also investigate peer exclusion and ensure an appropriate adult response rather than placing responsibility solely on the child. A visual organizer and specific feedback may support written expression while the learning goal remains clear. Progress should include both learning performance and meaningful participation, with the child’s perspective informing revisions.'},sources:[HOSA,CDC,PSY]
});

lessons.push({
title:'Adolescence: puberty, identity, peers and supported decisions',
hook:'A teenager can explain the risks of an activity in class yet makes a different choice when friends are watching. Knowledge matters, but decisions also depend on context, emotion, belonging and opportunities to pause.',
objectives:['Distinguish puberty from broader adolescent development.','Explain identity exploration and changing peer relationships.','Connect decision contexts with executive skills and emotional demands.','Support autonomy, privacy and help-seeking without stereotyping teenagers.'],
sections:[
['Puberty is a process, not a personality',[
'Puberty involves hormonal changes and the development of reproductive maturity, along with changes in growth, body composition and secondary sex characteristics. The timing and pace vary, and different changes do not occur simultaneously. Adolescence includes these physical changes but also broader cognitive, emotional and social development. Puberty therefore cannot explain every behavior a teenager displays. A useful case analysis identifies the particular change and its possible effect rather than treating “hormones” as a complete explanation.',
'Being earlier or later than peers can affect how a young person experiences their body and social environment. The impact depends on relationships, expectations and support. Avoid public comparisons or implying that one normal variation is superior. Concerns about development deserve a respectful clinical discussion when appropriate. Body image can be influenced by peers, advertising and online content; appearance-based comments may intensify distress even when intended as praise. Support should emphasize function, well-being and the person’s own concerns.',
'Physical growth can change energy needs, coordination and the experience of activities. A teenager may need to adjust to a changing body while navigating new expectations. Sleep timing also tends to shift during adolescence, and social schedules can conflict with adequate rest. A pattern of fatigue should prompt questions about sleep opportunities, routines, health and demands rather than immediate assumptions about discipline or motivation.'] ],
['Thinking, identity and the social world',[
'Adolescents become increasingly able to consider possibilities, reflect on ideas and question assumptions. Abstract reasoning develops unevenly and is influenced by familiarity, education and emotional context. A teenager may reason carefully about a hypothetical problem but struggle to apply that reasoning during a socially charged moment. This difference does not mean the earlier explanation was dishonest. It indicates that knowing a principle and using it under pressure are different demands.',
'Identity exploration can involve values, interests, community membership, future roles and relationships. Erikson’s identity-versus-role-confusion lens draws attention to the work of developing a coherent sense of self. Exploration is not automatically pathology or rebellion. Young people may try activities, reconsider goals and integrate multiple identities. Adults can provide structure and opportunities for reflection without demanding that every preference become a permanent commitment. A stable relationship can remain supportive while allowing disagreement.',
'Peers can offer friendship, information, encouragement and opportunities for contribution. Peer influence can support healthy behavior as well as risk. Joining a study group or speaking up against harassment also involves social influence. Families continue to matter even as peer relationships become more prominent. Avoid a false choice between complete adult control and complete independence; supported autonomy allows increasing responsibility while preserving access to guidance and help.'] ],
['Decision-making in context',[
'Developmental neuroscience highlights continuing changes in systems involved in planning, reward and social processing. These findings describe broad patterns; they do not establish that every adolescent is incapable of responsible decisions or that the brain changes abruptly at one exact birthday. Context matters. Time pressure, fatigue, intense emotion and the presence of peers can alter a decision. A calm discussion afterward may reveal reasoning that was difficult to use during the event.',
'A practical support strategy changes the decision environment. Before an event, identify likely pressure points, plan a way to leave and decide whom to contact. Practice a short response to an unwanted request. These steps make the safer action easier to retrieve when attention is narrowed. The goal is to build skills and options rather than simply repeat consequences. Positive risk-taking, such as trying a challenging activity or speaking to someone new, can support growth when the situation is reasonably safe.',
'Digital environments add rapid feedback, public comparison and persistent records. A young person may understand privacy in principle but underestimate how far a message can spread. Discuss audience, permanence, consent and the emotional effects of online interactions. Do not assume all digital use is harmful or that removing a device addresses every problem. Ask about the actual experience: connection, learning, exclusion, sleep disruption or pressure. A useful plan responds to the specific pattern.'] ],
['Communication, health and help-seeking',[
'Respectful adolescent communication includes listening directly to the young person, using clear language and inviting questions. Privacy can support honest discussion, but confidentiality has limits that depend on law, setting and safety responsibilities. Explain those limits rather than promising absolute secrecy. Consent and confidentiality rules vary; a general developmental lesson cannot substitute for current local requirements. Encourage involvement of trusted supportive adults when appropriate while recognizing that not every relationship is safe.',
'Persistent changes in mood, functioning, eating, sleep or social participation deserve attention rather than dismissal as ordinary teenage behavior. The task is to notice a pattern, ask respectfully and connect the person with appropriate support. A short scenario cannot diagnose a mental-health condition. Similarly, one disagreement does not prove a crisis. Evaluate severity, persistence, impairment and immediate safety concerns with qualified help when needed.',
sort('Separate supportive guidance from stereotypes',['Supportive guidance','Unhelpful assumption'],[['Practice a way to leave a pressured situation.',0],['Every teenager is incapable of reasoning.',1],['Ask how online interactions affect sleep and belonging.',0],['All disagreement proves pathology.',1],['Explain privacy and its limits clearly.',0],['Peer relationships only cause harm.',1]]),
flip('Plan before the pressure',[['The teenager knows a risk but struggles with peers present.','Practice an exit plan and a response before the situation; knowledge alone may not be available under pressure.'],['A young person changes a career goal.','Explore the reasons and what was learned; reconsideration can be part of identity development.'],['A teen appears constantly exhausted.','Ask about sleep opportunities, schedules, stress and health rather than assuming laziness.'],['A teen asks for confidentiality.','Explain what can remain private and the relevant limits before sensitive discussion.']]),
'In the opening case, compare conditions: classroom reasoning occurred with time and low social stakes, while the later choice occurred with an audience. Describe how preparation, supportive peers and an easier exit could change the situation. This explanation respects the teenager’s developing capabilities while recognizing that decision-making is shaped by the environment.'] ]],
takeaways:['Puberty is one component of adolescence.','Peers can support growth as well as risk.','Supported autonomy combines increasing responsibility with reliable guidance.'],terms:[['Identity exploration','Examining values, roles and affiliations while developing a sense of self.'],['Supported autonomy','Increasing choice and responsibility with appropriate structure and help.']],
mcqs:[q('Which statement distinguishes puberty from adolescence?','Puberty is a biological process within broader developmental change','The terms refer only to identical physical changes','Adolescence includes no cognitive changes','Puberty determines every behavior','Adolescence includes social, emotional and cognitive development as well as puberty.'),q('A teen reasons well in class but differently under peer pressure. What is the best interpretation?','Decision context can alter the use of knowledge','The teen cannot ever reason','The classroom answer proves deliberate deception','Knowledge has no value','Pressure, emotion and social rewards can change performance.'),q('Which example is positive peer influence?','Friends encourage one another to seek help and study','All peer activity is negative','Only adults can encourage safe behavior','Peers cannot influence learning','Social influence can support constructive choices.'),q('Which response supports identity exploration?','Ask what the young person learned from trying a new role','Demand a permanent career choice immediately','Treat every changed interest as illness','Forbid all reflection','Exploration can inform a coherent sense of self.'),q('What is a useful pre-event decision strategy?','Practice an exit plan and a brief refusal response','Rely only on a long lecture afterward','Assume risk knowledge guarantees behavior','Remove every opportunity for choice','Preparation makes a useful action more accessible under pressure.'),q('How should confidentiality be discussed?','Explain privacy and its limits clearly','Promise secrecy in every circumstance','Avoid mentioning any limits','Assume the same rule applies everywhere','Legal and safety responsibilities require transparent limits.'),q('Which pattern warrants further attention?','Persistent distress with declining functioning','Any new hobby','Every disagreement about clothing','Any preference for peers','Persistence and impairment distinguish a concern from ordinary variation.'),q('Which claim about brain development should be avoided?','Every person becomes fully mature on one exact birthday','Planning abilities continue changing over time','Context affects decisions','Experience can support learning','Population-level developmental patterns do not create a universal birthday threshold.')],
frq:{prompt:'A teenager understands an activity’s risks but participates when friends are present. Explain the discrepancy and design a plan that supports autonomy and safer decisions.',rubric:[['Explain context',['peer','pressure','emotion']],['Offer practical preparation',['plan','practice','exit']],['Respect developing autonomy',['choice','support','responsibility']]],model:'Classroom reasoning and a socially pressured decision impose different demands. Peer approval, emotion and limited time can make it harder to use known information. I would involve the teenager in identifying likely pressure points, practicing a brief response and planning a safe way to leave or contact support. This preserves meaningful choice while making a safer action easier to use. I would avoid claiming that all teenagers are irrational or that one birthday creates mature judgment.'},sources:[HOSA,TEEN,PSY]
});

lessons.push({
title:'Adulthood: relationships, work, health and adaptation',
hook:'Two adults of the same age describe very different priorities: one is starting training, while the other is caring for a parent and raising a child. A lifespan perspective recognizes multiple pathways rather than requiring one timetable for marriage, work or family.',
objectives:['Explain early and middle adulthood across developmental domains.','Apply intimacy and generativity without restricting them to one family pattern.','Distinguish changes in processing from accumulated knowledge.','Analyze role transitions, health behaviors and practical supports.'],
sections:[
['Adult development is not a fixed schedule',[
'Adulthood includes continuing physical, cognitive, social and emotional change. Educational opportunities, employment conditions, relationships, health and cultural expectations shape the sequence of roles. Chronological age alone does not reveal whether someone has financial independence, caregiving responsibilities or a stable home. A person can begin a profession later in life, return to education or establish new relationships after a major loss. These pathways are not necessarily delays or failures; the meaning depends on the person’s goals and circumstances.',
'Physical capacities can change gradually, but there is substantial variation related to health, activity, environment and prior experiences. Some functions may become more effortful while skills and strategies improve. Adult development should not be described as either uninterrupted decline or permanent stability. Reproductive transitions, illness, disability and changes in work demands can affect daily life in different ways. A useful assessment asks what changed, how it affects function and what the person wants to maintain or improve.',
'Age-related expectations can create pressure. An adult who does not want children may still have strong commitments and social contributions. Someone living with relatives may be participating in a valued family arrangement rather than failing to become independent. Avoid interpreting a life pattern against one culturally narrow template. Ask about autonomy, relationships, obligations and satisfaction in the person’s own context.'] ],
['Intimacy, contribution and changing roles',[
'Erikson’s intimacy-versus-isolation framework concerns the capacity for reciprocal closeness and commitment. Intimacy can occur through close friendships, partnerships and other trusted relationships; it is not measured by marital status alone. Healthy closeness allows both connection and individual identity. Isolation can reflect lack of opportunity, relationship loss or barriers to participation rather than an inherent inability to connect. A person who lives alone may have a rich social network, while someone living with others may feel lonely.',
'Generativity involves investing in people, projects or institutions that extend beyond oneself. Parenting is one expression, but mentoring, teaching, community work, creative contributions and care for others also qualify. Stagnation in the model refers to a sense of limited growth or contribution, not an objective judgment that one occupation or family structure lacks value. Ask what gives the person purpose and whether the environment allows them to act on it.',
'Roles can create both meaning and strain. An adult caring for a parent may feel connected and overwhelmed at the same time. Work, education, caregiving and household responsibilities can compete for time and resources. Role conflict occurs when expectations are difficult to satisfy simultaneously. A support plan may involve sharing tasks, adjusting schedules or connecting with services. Simply advising better time management can miss structural constraints such as inflexible employment or unavailable respite.'] ],
['Thinking, expertise and learning',[
'Adult cognition includes many abilities rather than one global capacity. **Fluid abilities** involve solving novel problems and manipulating information; **crystallized abilities** involve accumulated knowledge and experience. These categories summarize tendencies and do not predict every individual’s performance. Expertise can allow a person to recognize patterns quickly in a familiar domain even when an unfamiliar task takes longer. A clinician, mechanic or teacher may use organized knowledge to avoid unnecessary steps that a novice must consider individually.',
'Learning remains possible throughout adulthood. Effective instruction connects new material with prior knowledge, makes the purpose clear and offers practice with feedback. Adults may need flexible formats because of work, caregiving or accessibility needs. A slow response may reflect unfamiliar software, hearing difficulty, anxiety or competing demands rather than an inability to learn. Break a complex procedure into manageable parts and observe which part creates difficulty.',
'Decision-making often integrates experience, values and uncertainty. A person choosing between jobs may weigh income, meaning, transport, health and family responsibilities. There may be no single objectively best choice. Developmental reasoning recognizes the tradeoff and asks whether the person understands the options and can act on their priorities. It should not substitute the observer’s preferred life path for the adult’s own values.'] ],
['Health promotion and transitions',[
'Health promotion in adulthood combines habits, preventive care, supportive environments and management of existing conditions. Recommendations should be individualized and drawn from current clinical guidance when specific screening schedules or treatments are involved. A developmental lesson can explain why access, routine and self-management matter without prescribing a universal medical plan. Opportunities for movement, rest and social connection depend partly on work and household conditions, so practical feasibility should be assessed.',
'Transitions can include becoming a caregiver, changing jobs, migration, separation, retirement planning or living with a new diagnosis. Even a desired transition may involve loss of familiar routines and identity. Adaptation may require learning new skills, renegotiating relationships and accessing resources. Do not assume that every middle-aged adult experiences a dramatic crisis. The popular “midlife crisis” narrative is not a universal stage through which everyone must pass.',
match('Match the adult-development concept',[['Using extensive professional knowledge to solve a familiar problem','Crystallized ability and expertise'],['Learning a novel problem-solving rule','Fluid reasoning demands'],['Maintaining reciprocal closeness','Intimacy'],['Mentoring a younger colleague','Generativity'],['Conflicting work and caregiving expectations','Role conflict'],['Feeling alone despite living with others','Loneliness rather than household size']]),
sort('Evaluate the support plan',['Person-centered and feasible','Assumption-driven'],[['Ask which responsibility the adult most wants help with.',0],['Assume unmarried adults cannot experience intimacy.',1],['Offer learning formats compatible with caregiving.',0],['Call every career change a crisis.',1],['Consider schedule and transport before recommending a program.',0]]),
'In the opening comparison, begin with each adult’s actual goals. One may need flexible training and financial support; the other may need shared caregiving and respite. Both may be developing competence and contributing to others. A strong FRQ explains the person’s roles, identifies a mechanism of strain or growth and proposes a realistic support without presenting one sequence of adult milestones as mandatory.'] ]],
takeaways:['Adult pathways vary with goals, culture and opportunity.','Intimacy and generativity are broader than marriage and parenthood.','Learning and adaptation continue even as some capacities change.'],terms:[['Generativity','Contribution to people or purposes beyond oneself.'],['Role conflict','Difficulty meeting competing expectations from different roles.']],
mcqs:[q('Which statement best reflects adult development?','People follow multiple pathways shaped by context and goals','All adults must reach roles in one order','Development stops after education','Age reveals every responsibility','Adult life trajectories are diverse.'),q('Which example demonstrates generativity?','Mentoring a new colleague','Avoiding every social contribution','Only becoming a biological parent','Only receiving a promotion','Generativity includes support for others and future contributions.'),q('What does intimacy refer to in Erikson’s framework?','Reciprocal closeness and commitment','Marital status alone','The number of people in a house','Complete loss of personal identity','Intimacy concerns relationship quality rather than one formal status.'),q('Which example most clearly uses crystallized ability?','Applying accumulated expertise to a familiar professional problem','Learning an entirely novel rule without prior knowledge','Measuring physical height','Responding to a sudden noise','Crystallized ability draws on learned knowledge.'),q('An adult struggles with work and caregiving schedules. What is emphasized?','Role conflict','A universal midlife crisis','Failure of all cognition','A fixed personality defect','Competing role expectations can create strain.'),q('Which teaching approach supports adult learning?','Connect material to prior knowledge and provide relevant practice','Assume adults cannot learn new skills','Use only one inaccessible format','Avoid explaining the purpose','Relevance, practice and access support learning.'),q('Which assumption should be avoided?','Every middle-aged person must experience a crisis','Transitions can require adaptation','Caregiving can bring both meaning and strain','People differ in their priorities','A midlife crisis is not a universal required stage.'),q('What is the best first step in a support plan?','Ask about the adult’s goals and practical constraints','Choose the professional’s preferred life path','Infer needs solely from age','Ignore existing strengths','Person-centered support begins with the individual’s priorities.')],
frq:{prompt:'An adult returns to school while caring for a parent and feels guilty about reduced time with friends. Apply adult-development concepts and propose two realistic supports.',rubric:[['Explain roles and strain',['role','caregiving','conflict']],['Recognize growth and connection',['learning','intimacy','generativity']],['Offer feasible supports',['schedule','respite','support']]],model:'The adult is managing competing roles rather than necessarily experiencing a developmental failure. Returning to school can support growth, while caregiving can express generativity and also create strain. Reduced social time may threaten valued intimacy or connection. I would ask which goals matter most and explore flexible learning arrangements and shared caregiving or respite if available. The plan should preserve meaningful relationships in a workable way and be reviewed as responsibilities change.'},sources:[HOSA,PSY,AGE]
});

lessons.push({
title:'Aging: function, cognition, independence and connection',
hook:'An older adult takes longer to answer a question in a noisy room but gives a detailed, accurate response when the room is quiet. Before concluding that cognition has declined, consider hearing, environment, time and the person’s baseline.',
objectives:['Distinguish aging-related variation from changes requiring assessment.','Describe sensory, physical and cognitive factors affecting function.','Differentiate delirium, dementia and ordinary lapses conceptually.','Support autonomy, accessible communication and social participation.'],
sections:[
['Aging and the diversity of function',[
'Older adulthood is highly diverse. Chronological age does not reveal a person’s strength, knowledge, health, relationships or ability to make decisions. Biological changes interact with accumulated experiences, illness, access to care and the environment. Some people need extensive support, while others remain independent in complex activities. Describing all older adults as frail or dependent is inaccurate and can reduce opportunities. Assess the specific task and the assistance required rather than inferring overall capacity from appearance or age.',
'Age-associated changes can include reduced physiological reserve, changes in skin and body composition, and alterations in sensory function. Reserve describes the capacity to respond to extra demands, such as illness or exertion. A person may function well in familiar routines but become more vulnerable during a major stressor. This does not mean decline is inevitable in every domain or that intervention is pointless. Movement, rehabilitation, accessible environments and management of health conditions may support function.',
'Distinguish a symptom from an explanation. A fall is an event to investigate, not simply “old age.” Possible contributors include environmental hazards, vision, balance, medication effects and an acute illness. Similarly, unexplained weight loss, new confusion or a marked change in function should not be dismissed as normal aging. A learner’s role is to recognize the need for assessment rather than assign a diagnosis from a short description.'] ],
['Sensation, communication and daily activities',[
'Changes in hearing and vision can affect communication and performance. A person who misses a spoken instruction may appear forgetful when the instruction was not heard clearly. Reduce background noise, face the person, use clear speech and check whether an assistive device is available and working. Avoid shouting automatically, using childish language or directing every question to a companion. Speak to the person first and ask how they prefer to receive information.',
'**Activities of daily living** include basic self-care such as dressing, bathing and eating. **Instrumental activities of daily living** include more complex tasks such as shopping, managing transport, preparing meals or organizing finances. Difficulty in one task does not establish inability in every task. A person may need help with transport while independently managing decisions and self-care. Assessment should identify the specific barrier and preserve the abilities that remain effective.',
'The environment can compensate for difficulty. Better lighting, clear labels, accessible storage and a predictable routine may reduce unnecessary effort. An assistive device is not evidence of personal failure; it can increase autonomy. Ask which activities the person values before recommending changes. A technically safe arrangement that removes all meaningful participation may not meet the person’s goals. Shared planning considers safety, preference and practical alternatives rather than treating independence as an all-or-nothing state.'] ],
['Cognition: careful distinctions',[
'Some people experience slower processing or occasional difficulty retrieving a word while retaining knowledge and effective daily function. Such experiences differ from a persistent decline that interferes with independence. **Dementia** is a syndrome involving significant cognitive impairment that affects functioning; it is not an inevitable part of aging and is not synonymous with one disease. Alzheimer’s disease is one cause among several. A diagnosis requires clinical evaluation rather than a casual memory quiz.',
'**Delirium** involves an acute disturbance in attention and awareness, often with fluctuation. It can occur with illness, medication effects or other physiological problems and requires prompt medical assessment. It may occur in someone who also has dementia. The key contrast is not simply “young versus old” or “reversible versus irreversible,” but the pattern of onset, attention, course and underlying causes. Sudden change from baseline should be taken seriously.',
'Mood, sleep, pain, hearing and medications can also affect thinking and participation. Depression is not a normal requirement of aging, and a person’s withdrawal should not automatically be interpreted as dementia. Gather the timeline, functional effects and information from the person and appropriate supporters. Use the least assumptive description: “new difficulty following a conversation over two days” is more informative than “senile.” Respectful language improves reasoning because it keeps the observed change separate from an unsupported label.'] ],
['Connection, adaptation and autonomy',[
'Social isolation describes limited contact or participation; loneliness is the subjective feeling of insufficient connection. They overlap but are not identical. An older adult may prefer substantial time alone without feeling lonely, while another may feel disconnected in a busy household. Ask about desired relationships and activities rather than assuming that more contact is always the answer. Transport, hearing access, grief and digital barriers can influence the ability to maintain valued connections.',
'Retirement, relocation, illness and caregiving changes can affect identity and routine. Adaptation may involve selecting priorities, using effective strategies and compensating for a difficulty. For example, a person may focus on fewer valued activities, practice them at a comfortable time and use transport assistance. This is active adjustment rather than simply surrendering independence. Erikson’s integrity-versus-despair lens can help explain life review, but it should not be used to demand optimism or deny legitimate regret.',
sort('Distinguish the pattern',['Needs prompt assessment','Possible ordinary variation or access issue—clarify'],[['New fluctuating inattention over a day',0],['Difficulty hearing instructions in a noisy room',1],['Sudden major change from usual function',0],['Occasional word retrieval difficulty without functional decline',1],['Repeated unexplained falls',0]]),
match('Match the term',[['Basic tasks such as bathing and dressing','Activities of daily living'],['Complex tasks such as shopping and finances','Instrumental activities of daily living'],['Acute fluctuating disturbance in attention','Delirium'],['Cognitive decline interfering with independent function','Dementia syndrome'],['Feeling insufficiently connected','Loneliness'],['Limited social contact','Social isolation']]),
'In the opening case, the improved response in quiet conditions argues for considering access before making a global cognitive judgment. It does not rule out every other concern. Establish the baseline, address the environment and seek appropriate assessment if difficulties persist or change. The objective is accurate understanding that protects both safety and the person’s right to participate in decisions.'] ]],
takeaways:['Age alone does not establish capacity or dependence.','New or sudden functional changes require attention.','Accessible environments can preserve participation and autonomy.'],terms:[['Delirium','An acute, often fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness requiring clinical assessment.'],['Instrumental activities of daily living','Complex tasks that support independent community living.']],
mcqs:[q('What is the strongest first response to difficulty answering in a noisy room?','Improve communication access and clarify the baseline','Diagnose dementia immediately','Speak only to the companion','Assume the person cannot decide','Sensory and environmental barriers can affect performance.'),q('Which task is an instrumental activity of daily living?','Managing household finances','Swallowing food','Bathing','Putting on a shirt','Instrumental tasks are more complex activities supporting independent living.'),q('Which pattern most strongly raises concern for delirium?','Sudden fluctuating inattention','A lifelong preference for quiet','One forgotten name years ago','Stable knowledge over many years','Acute onset and fluctuating attention warrant prompt assessment.'),q('Which statement about dementia is accurate?','It is not an inevitable part of aging','It is identical to every ordinary memory lapse','It can be diagnosed from age alone','It always begins suddenly over hours','Dementia involves significant impairment and requires evaluation.'),q('What distinguishes loneliness from isolation?','Loneliness is subjective; isolation concerns contact or participation','They always mean exactly the same thing','Living alone always proves both','Neither affects development','Desired connection and actual contact are related but different.'),q('Which approach preserves autonomy?','Identify specific support needs while retaining effective abilities','Take over every task after one difficulty','Assume assistive devices remove independence','Ignore the person’s preferences','Support should be targeted and person-centered.'),q('Why should an unexplained fall be assessed?','Several modifiable or acute factors may contribute','Falls are always an unavoidable age effect','A fall proves one specific disease','No intervention can affect safety','An event requires investigation rather than an age-based explanation.'),q('Which communication style is best?','Speak directly and respectfully with appropriate access supports','Use childish language','Automatically shout','Exclude the person from discussion','Respect and accessibility improve understanding and participation.')],
frq:{prompt:'An older adult has new fluctuating confusion and difficulty managing meals after an illness. Explain why this should not be dismissed as aging and identify information and supports needed.',rubric:[['Recognize acute change',['acute','fluctuating','delirium']],['Gather baseline and causes',['baseline','illness','medication']],['Preserve function and autonomy',['support','meal','preference']]],model:'The acute, fluctuating change raises concern for delirium or another medical problem and warrants prompt clinical assessment. It should not be labeled normal aging or assumed to be dementia. I would clarify the person’s usual attention and function, the illness timeline and relevant medication or sensory issues. Temporary help with meals may be needed, but the plan should preserve abilities and choices the person can still exercise. Support should be reviewed as the underlying problem is assessed and treated.'},sources:[HOSA,AGE,['MedlinePlus: Delirium','https://medlineplus.gov/delirium.html']]
});

lessons.push({
title:'End of life, loss and grief: care without a fixed script',
hook:'After the same family death, one person cries openly, another concentrates on practical tasks and a child asks whether the person will return tomorrow. Different expressions do not establish how much each person cared.',
objectives:['Distinguish grief, mourning and bereavement.','Explain why stage descriptions are not a required sequence.','Connect developmental understanding with communication about death.','Describe person-centered end-of-life support and indications for additional help.'],
sections:[
['Understanding loss and its expression',[
'**Bereavement** is the situation of having experienced a death. **Grief** includes emotional, cognitive, physical and social responses to loss. **Mourning** refers to ways grief is expressed through personal and cultural practices. Grief can also follow losses other than death, such as loss of health, a relationship or an anticipated future. Anticipatory grief may occur before an expected death or major loss. These terms distinguish experiences without assigning a single correct emotional response.',
'People may experience sadness, anger, relief, guilt, numbness or combinations that change over time. Relief after a long period of suffering does not prove lack of love. Concentrating on paperwork does not prove denial. Cultural and family practices influence what is expressed publicly, who participates in rituals and how support is offered. Ask what is meaningful to the person rather than assuming that one ritual or style of disclosure will fit everyone.',
'The relationship, circumstances of death, prior losses, available support and practical consequences all shape the experience. A death may create financial or caregiving changes alongside emotional pain. A plan that offers only conversation may miss an urgent need for meals, transport or childcare. Conversely, practical help does not eliminate the need for emotional acknowledgment. Good support follows the person’s needs as they evolve.'] ],
['Why grief does not follow a mandatory staircase',[
'The familiar terms denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance come from a historically influential model associated with Kübler-Ross. They should not be taught as universal stages that every bereaved person must experience in a fixed order. People may recognize some experiences, none of them or repeated shifts among different responses. Using the model as a timetable can make people feel that they are grieving incorrectly and can cause observers to overlook what the person actually needs.',
'Other approaches describe grief as involving ongoing adaptation to life after loss. People may alternate between attending to the loss and dealing with practical tasks or new roles. Continuing bonds, such as remembering, telling stories or maintaining meaningful traditions, can coexist with adaptation. Moving forward does not necessarily require forgetting or severing every connection with the person who died. The important question is how the person is functioning and whether the relationship to the loss is becoming manageable in their context.',
'Distinguish supportive observation from diagnosis. Persistent, severe distress or marked interference with daily life may warrant professional assessment, but a student should not diagnose a disorder based solely on the calendar or one emotional expression. Cultural expectations and the nature of the loss matter. Immediate safety concerns require immediate support through appropriate local services. Less urgent concerns still deserve attention; the absence of a crisis does not mean the person should manage alone.'] ],
['Developmental communication about death',[
'Children’s understanding of death changes with cognitive development and experience. Relevant concepts include irreversibility, the ending of bodily functions and universality. A younger child may ask the same question repeatedly or interpret explanations literally. Avoid euphemisms such as “went to sleep,” which can create confusion about sleep or return. Use clear, age-appropriate language, give manageable information and invite questions. Do not force an adult-style conversation when play or drawing provides another way to communicate.',
'Children may wonder whether an angry thought or a disagreement caused the death. Reassure them accurately that ordinary thoughts and feelings did not cause it. Changes in behavior, play, sleep or school participation may communicate distress even when the child says little. Familiar routines and reliable caregivers can provide continuity, but routines should not be used to prohibit expression. Participation in rituals should be explained and supported in a way that fits the child and family rather than imposed without preparation.',
'Adolescents may understand the permanence of death while facing peer, identity and privacy concerns. Adults may be balancing grief with employment or caregiving. Older adults may experience several losses in a short period, but familiarity with death does not make a new loss unimportant. Across ages, ask what the person understands, correct misconceptions gently and avoid assuming that chronological age determines the intensity or meaning of grief.'] ],
['Person-centered end-of-life support',[
'Palliative care focuses on relief of symptoms, quality of life and support for people with serious illness and their families. It can accompany disease-directed treatment and is not limited to the final days of life. Hospice is a particular model of care for people approaching the end of life, with eligibility and coverage rules that vary by system. Avoid teaching the two terms as interchangeable. The relevant developmental principle is care that follows goals, values and changing needs.',
'Advance care planning involves discussing preferences, identifying a person who may speak for the patient when needed and documenting decisions through appropriate processes. A document is useful but does not replace conversation. Preferences may change as the situation becomes clearer. Decision-making capacity is task-specific and should not be assumed absent because of age, disability or disagreement with a professional. Local legal requirements must be checked when applying consent or advance-directive procedures.',
sort('Supportive or prescriptive?',['Supportive response','Unhelpful prescription'],[['Ask which practical help would be useful this week.',0],['You must reach acceptance by this date.',1],['Use clear language when explaining death to a child.',0],['People who do not cry did not care.',1],['Offer space for memories and changing feelings.',0],['Palliative care means all other care must stop.',1]]),
match('Distinguish the terms',[['The situation following a death','Bereavement'],['Responses to loss','Grief'],['Personal and cultural expression of grief','Mourning'],['Grief before an expected loss','Anticipatory grief'],['Support for symptoms and quality of life during serious illness','Palliative care']]),
'In the opening family, begin by asking rather than ranking reactions. The person handling tasks may need respite; the person crying may want companionship; the child may need a clear explanation and repeated reassurance. Offer specific help and check back over time. A strong HOSA response describes the developmental understanding, respects culture and avoids imposing a universal sequence on the experience.'] ]],
takeaways:['Grief has no mandatory sequence or universal timetable.','Clear, developmentally appropriate communication reduces confusion.','End-of-life support follows goals, symptoms and the person’s values.'],terms:[['Bereavement','The situation of having experienced a death.'],['Mourning','Personal and cultural expression of grief.']],
mcqs:[q('Which term describes the situation after a death?','Bereavement','Reinforcement','Accommodation','Maturation','Bereavement describes the loss situation; grief describes responses.'),q('Which statement about grief stages is accurate?','They are not a required universal sequence','Everyone must experience them in order','Failure to cry proves denial','Acceptance must occur by a fixed date','Stage labels should not become a mandatory timetable.'),q('Why avoid telling a young child that a person “went to sleep”?','Literal interpretation can create confusion about sleep and return','Children never understand direct language','Euphemisms always prevent distress','The child should receive no explanation','Clear language helps distinguish death from ordinary sleep.'),q('Which reaction can occur without proving lack of love?','Relief after a prolonged period of suffering','Only constant crying','Only anger','Only public disclosure','Grief can contain mixed emotions, including relief.'),q('What is anticipatory grief?','Grief before an expected loss','A fixed stage after acceptance','Proof that death is certain on a specific date','A lack of attachment','Anticipatory grief can occur before an expected death or other loss.'),q('Which description of palliative care is correct?','It supports quality of life and can accompany disease-directed care','It always means all treatment stops','It is identical to every hospice program','It is only for the final hour of life','Palliative care is broader than final-days care.'),q('What makes an additional assessment appropriate?','Severe persistent distress or marked functional difficulty','Any culturally meaningful ritual','Any memory of the deceased','Any day without tears','Intensity, impairment and context matter more than one expression.'),q('Which support is most person-centered?','Ask about emotional and practical needs and follow up','Require the same ritual for every family','Rank relatives by how much they cry','Assume age determines how much help is needed','Needs differ and change over time.')],
frq:{prompt:'A child repeatedly asks when a deceased relative will return, while an adult family member feels guilty about relief after a long illness. Explain two developmentally appropriate responses and one misconception to avoid.',rubric:[['Communicate clearly with the child',['clear','death','return']],['Validate mixed adult emotions',['relief','guilt','emotion']],['Avoid fixed grief stages',['sequence','stage','timetable']]],model:'I would explain to the child in clear, age-appropriate language that the relative died and will not return, invite repeated questions and maintain reliable support. I would avoid saying the person went to sleep. For the adult, I would acknowledge that relief after prolonged suffering can coexist with love and sadness, and ask what support would help. Neither reaction should be judged against a fixed grief-stage sequence or deadline. Persistent severe distress or functional difficulties would justify professional assessment in context.'},sources:[HOSA,GRIEF,['National Institute on Aging: End of life','https://www.nia.nih.gov/health/end-life']]
});

// Additional evidence exercises make each unit connect observation with a mechanism.
lessons[2].sources.push(['OpenStax: Lifespan theories','https://openstax.org/books/psychology/pages/9-2-lifespan-theories']);
lessons[8].sources.push(['National Eye Institute: Age-related macular degeneration','https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/age-related-macular-degeneration']);
lessons[0].sections.push(['Read developmental evidence carefully',[
'A cross-sectional study compares people of different ages at one time; a longitudinal study follows the same people over time. Cross-sectional differences can reflect cohort experiences, such as different schooling or technology exposure, as well as age. Longitudinal findings can be affected by practice, dropout and historical events that occur during the study. Neither design automatically establishes cause. If older participants perform differently on a computer task, ask whether the result reflects the intended cognitive skill, familiarity with the device or unequal access. The same principle applies to everyday assessment: make sure the observation measures the capacity you intend to examine rather than an unrelated obstacle.'
]]);
lessons[1].sections.push(['Separate a need from a preferred solution',[
'A family may say that they need a particular program when the underlying goal is reliable childcare or help understanding an evaluation. Clarifying the goal creates room for several solutions if the first option is unavailable. Document the person’s priority, the identified barrier, the agreed action and who will follow up. Avoid collecting sensitive information that is unnecessary for the referral. If an agency cannot provide the service, explain the next available step rather than leaving the family with an unexplained rejection. This distinction between need, resource and outcome makes coordination more transparent and reduces repeated referrals that do not lead to usable help.'
]]);
lessons[2].sections.push(['Compare predictions in one learning task',[
'Imagine teaching a child to organize materials for school. A cognitive explanation asks whether the child understands categories and sequence. A social-learning explanation asks what models the child has observed. An operant explanation examines whether consequences make the organizing behavior more likely. A sociocultural explanation asks which prompts, language and shared routines support performance. An ecological explanation considers space, household schedules and school expectations. These accounts are not interchangeable, but several may be relevant at once. A useful prediction is testable: if a visible sequence reduces forgotten items, that supports the usefulness of an external planning aid without proving that every other explanation is false.'
]]);
lessons[3].sections.push(['Genes, chromosomes and uncertainty',[
'Genes are segments of DNA that contribute to biological processes; chromosomes organize DNA into larger structures. Inherited variation, new genetic changes and environmental influences can all affect development. A genetic finding does not always predict the same severity or experience in every person. Genetic counseling helps people interpret testing options, limitations and possible results in relation to their values. Keep a family history distinct from a diagnosis: it may identify a reason to ask further questions without establishing that a fetus has a condition. Likewise, a reassuring screening result reduces some concerns but does not guarantee every aspect of future health or development.'
]]);
lessons[4].sections.push(['Document the conditions of a developmental observation',[
'Write down whether the child was rested, comfortable, familiar with the materials and given an opportunity to respond. A caregiver’s report of a skill at home may differ from what appears during a short clinic visit, and both observations can be useful. For a child born preterm, clinicians may consider corrected age when interpreting early development; the appropriate comparison should follow the child’s professional guidance rather than an improvised calculation. Note whether a skill is emerging, consistently demonstrated or reported in one setting only. This richer record supports discussion about progress and concerns more effectively than a single label such as “ahead” or “behind.”'
]]);
lessons[5].sections.push(['Make practice informative',[
'Practice is most useful when it reveals the next learning step. A child who repeatedly guesses an answer may need a worked demonstration rather than more identical questions. Ask the child to describe a strategy, then try a related example with one feature changed. This distinguishes memorized performance from transferable understanding. Space practice across several opportunities and return to an earlier skill after a delay. A mistake can reveal a misconception, an attention lapse or an unclear instruction; the response should depend on which explanation is supported. Recording the type of error is therefore more informative than counting errors alone, especially when planning individualized support.'
]]);
lessons[6].sections.push(['Negotiate responsibility with observable agreements',[
'A family disagreement about independence can become more productive when expectations are concrete. Instead of “be more responsible,” agree on a plan for transport, communication and what to do if circumstances change. The adolescent should help shape the plan so that it is understood and realistic. Review what worked afterward and expand responsibility when skills and conditions support it. If an agreement fails, separate the outcome from the person’s identity: investigate whether the problem involved planning, pressure, access or an unclear expectation. Accountability can coexist with curiosity. The aim is to develop increasingly reliable judgment, not to win every disagreement through authority.'
]]);
lessons[7].sections.push(['Adaptation through selection and compensation',[
'Adults often maintain effective performance by selecting priorities, optimizing useful skills and compensating for a difficulty. A worker returning after illness might focus first on essential tasks, practice an efficient routine and use a checklist for a complex sequence. This does not imply that every barrier can be solved by individual effort; workload and workplace access may also need adjustment. Distinguish the desired outcome from the old method of achieving it. Maintaining a valued role through a different strategy can be successful adaptation. Evaluate whether the change supports the person’s goals, reduces unnecessary strain and remains practical across the actual demands of daily life.'
]]);
lessons[8].sections.push(['Use patterns of sensory change to ask better questions',[
'Vision complaints should be described specifically. Difficulty focusing on nearby print, reduced clarity, problems with glare and loss in part of the visual field are different experiences. For example, age-related macular degeneration affects central vision, which can interfere with reading or recognizing faces; that pattern differs from a general claim that all vision is equally reduced. A developmental student should recognize the distinction without diagnosing the cause. Ask about onset, functional effects and whether professional assessment has occurred. Sudden changes require prompt clinical attention. Adaptations such as improved contrast or accessible text may support participation while appropriate assessment and treatment decisions remain with qualified clinicians.'
]]);
lessons[9].sections.push(['Support the supporter',[
'Family caregivers and professionals can also experience grief, exhaustion and conflicting responsibilities. A specific offer—bringing a meal, handling a permitted errand or covering a routine task—may be easier to use than “call if you need anything.” Ask before taking over, because a familiar task may provide structure or meaning. Respect privacy when discussing the loss with others. Continue checking in after public rituals end, when support may decrease but practical changes remain. Remembering an anniversary or asking about a meaningful memory can acknowledge the continuing relationship without demanding disclosure. The person may welcome conversation on one day and prefer quiet companionship on another.'
]]);

export default course('Human Growth & Development',{
intro:'Study development as a lifelong interaction of biology, thinking, relationships and opportunity. This course follows the HOSA 2026–27 blueprint: government/culture/family 20%, theories 16%, prenatal/infancy 12%, childhood/adolescence 22%, adulthood 15%, and geriatrics/end of life/grief 15%. Approximate age patterns support reasoning; they are not diagnostic deadlines.',
units:[
{title:'Context and developmental explanations',summary:'Family, cultural and government influences, then the theories used to explain development.',lessons:lessons.slice(0,3)},
{title:'From conception through adolescence',summary:'Connected physical, cognitive and social-emotional changes with opportunities for supported learning.',lessons:lessons.slice(3,7)},
{title:'Adult lives, aging and loss',summary:'Diverse adult pathways, functional adaptation, autonomy and compassionate support.',lessons:lessons.slice(7)}
]});
